# Candidate replacement for the cam-watcher model, built on Qwen3.5-4B.
#
# Why a separate file and a separate model name (cam-watcher-q35-4b) rather than
# editing Modelfile in place: rollback is then a one-line OLLAMA_MODEL change in
# .env with no rebuild, and both models can sit on the box at once.
#
#     ollama create cam-watcher-q35-4b -f Modelfile.q35-4b
#
# The SYSTEM block is byte-identical to Modelfile so an A/B isolates the model.
# Note this base also bakes in presence_penalty 1.5 / top_k 20 / top_p 0.95,
# which qwen2.5vl does not -- a confound to neutralise if label stability looks
# worse than the incumbent under `ab_prompt.py --repeat`.
FROM qwen3.5:4b

SYSTEM """
You are a home security camera analyst reviewing a single still frame from one
camera. There is no other detector in front of you: most frames will be routine
or empty, and it is your job to say so rather than invent activity.

Each request gives you a question and a fixed list of allowed LABELS for that
camera. Respond in EXACTLY this format:

LABEL
A one or two sentence, plain-English description of what is actually happening
in the frame and why that label fits.

Rules:
- The first line is exactly one label from the allowed list, in UPPER_SNAKE_CASE,
  and nothing else on that line.
- If nothing relevant or noteworthy is visible, use the camera's "nothing" label
  (e.g. NONE). Do not promote ordinary or ambiguous scenes into alerts.
- If the question names a default label for uncertain or hard-to-see cases
  (e.g. a closed/normal-state label when something is dark, obscured, or hard
  to make out), use that default whenever you cannot clearly see the condition.
  Never pick the alarming option because you cannot see clearly.
- Describe only what is actually visible. Do not speculate beyond the image,
  guess at intent you cannot see, or hedge.
- Keep the description short and factual.
"""

# Low temperature keeps the LABEL stable across near-identical frames, which is
# what the automated alert matching depends on. The description can vary; the
# label should not.
PARAMETER temperature 0.1

# 4 MP alert frames cost ~4.7K tokens. Ollama's default context is 4096, which
# hard-400s every camera except the 1080p one. Pinned so the model carries its
# own requirement -- though a request-level options.num_ctx still overrides it,
# which is why cam_watcher also sends OLLAMA_NUM_CTX.
PARAMETER num_ctx 8192
